1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.ingebretsens.com/uraed-egg-crossed-the-atlantic.html "Uræd"-The Egg That Crossed the Atlantic]
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 7, 1904, Lars Madsen Salthammer and Karl Thomas Hagevik Johansen set out on an adventurous ocean voyage from Alesund with a course set for New York, to prove that enclosed lifeboats are the only solution for improving se
2 CONC afarers’ safety. It was a dramatic expedition across a stormy Atlantic Ocean. Five months later  the boat is thrown up onto Pavilion beach during a violent storm along the rocky Cape Ann coast near Gloucester in the U.S.A. The sub
2 CONC sequent years of struggle to acquire approval for the new type of lifeboast were stormy in their own right. This book, written out of respect and admiration for the four who put their lives on the line for the safety of seamen. By
2 CONC  the time the use of enclosed lifeboats finally saw a breakthrough, they had all passed away. This book tells the history of safety at sea and the fight for new lifeboat and raft types, followed all the way up to 2004, 100 years a
2 CONC fter the Uræd expedition.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.norwayheritage.com/snitz/topic.asp?TOPIC_ID=6295
