1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Rasmus was born September 23, 1872. He was the first of five children born to Ole Hansen Soras and Andrianna Jakobsdatter Leirvaag. The family story is that he was the first child to be christened in the “new” Vest
2 CONC nes church (11/24/1872). Twenty-nine years later he was married there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rasmus made his first trip to America in 1893 at the age of twenty-one. Before leaving home a young neighbor Anna Pauline Misfjord promised her hand in marriage when he returned. In America Rasmus worked for the Northern Pacific r
2 CONC ailroad for several years and then returned to Norway to marry Anna. They were married September 20, 1901. He was twenty-nine and she was twenty-eight. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On March 19, 1902 Rasmus, Anna, her brother Kristoffer and her younger sister Anne left for America on the ship Saxonia. They landed in New York on April 19, 1902. The trip was hard and Anna was pregnant with her first child Haral
2 CONC d. It was so bad she vowed never to return by boat, and she was afraid to fly so it never happened. They passed through Ellis Island and headed by train to Valley City in the red river valley of North Dakota. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne was a teenager (16) and lived with Rasmus and Anna for several years until she married Adolf Torgeson. Kristoffer continued on and was next seen when he was on his way home to Norway. This would have been about 1915. Alpha wa
2 CONC s about five at the time, and she claims to remember him passing through. He saved his money and bought the Misfjord farm near Vestnes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rasmus bought a twelve-volume encyclopedia set on carpentry and became a self-taught carpenter. His first big project was to build his home. He continued to build until he was over eighty when his children made him stop climbing. 
2 CONC He then specialized in cabinet making. It is said, however, these skills were in the family because his grandfather helped build the old bridge that crosses the fjord in Vestnes. At some point after arriving in America he changed 
2 CONC his name to Soroos. Sometimes, for business purposes, he used the name Rasmus Olsen Soroos. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Rasmus and Anna were charter members of the Old Savior’s Lutheran Church in Valley City. He helped build the church and for many years the services were held in Norwegian. Valley City was a community mainly of Norwegian immigrants
2 CONC . They had five children and none were given middle names. The three older children Harald, Esther and Adolf did not speak English until they started school. After they began school they taught English to the younger two, Ralph an
2 CONC d Alpha. Rasmus could read and write English because it was needed for business. Anna learned to speak English, but never learned to read or write very well.
2 CONT
2 CONT In general the Soroos’ adjusted to life in Valley City easily because the food and life were the same as before in Norway. Norwegian holidays were still observed. Rasmus died in the winter of 1958 in Valley City at the age of eigh
2 CONC ty-six and Anna passed away two years later. Her life without Rasmus was hard.
